body:
`expressionContainsErrorState()` does not handle `ChainExpression` (optional chaining). As a result, derived error variables like `const hasError = query.error?.message` / `const hasError = !!query.error?.message` won't be recognized as error state sources, so `derivedErrorVariables` won't be recorded and the rule may falsely report the underlying query/mutation as unhandled. Consider unwrapping `ChainExpression` (similar to `collectErrorStateFromExpression`) before checking `Identifier`/`MemberExpression`/etc.
```suggestion
    // Recursively checks identifiers, member expressions, chain expressions, logical expressions, conditional expressions, and unary expressions
    function expressionContainsErrorState(expr: any, visited = new Set<any>()): boolean {
      if (!expr) return false;

      if (expr.type === 'ChainExpression') {
        return expressionContainsErrorState(expr.expression, visited);
      }
```

